:"Chemical","mesh":"D000305","start":1023,"end":1037},{"pmid":"11058428","text":"myopathy","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009135","start":1038,"end":1046}],"gold_relations":"methylprednisolone | CID | myopathy","metrics":{"precision":0.3333333333333333,"recall":1.0,"f1":0.5,"tp":1,"fp":2,"fn":0,"rel_precision":0.3333333333333333,"rel_recall":1.0,"rel_f1":0.5,"rel_tp":1,"rel_fp":2,"rel_fn":0},"pmid":"11058428","relations":["D008775 | CID | D013119","D008775 | CID | D009133","D008775 | CID | D009135"],"text":"High-dose methylprednisolone may do more harm for spinal cord injury. Because of the National Acute Spinal Cord Injury Studies (NASCIS), high-dose methylprednisolone became the standard of care for the acute spinal cord injury. In the NASCIS, there was no mention regarding the possibility of acute corticosteroid myopathy that high-dose methylprednisolone may cause. The dosage of methylprednisolone recommended by the NASCIS 3 is the highest dose of steroids ever being used during a 2-day period for any clinical condition. We hypothesize that it may cause some damage to the muscle of spinal cord injury patients. Further, steroid myopathy recovers naturally and the neurological improvement shown in the NASCIS may be just a recording of this natural motor recovery from the steroid myopathy, instead of any protection that methylprednisolone offers to the spinal cord injury. To our knowledge, this is the first discussion considering the possibility that the methylprednisolone recommended by NASCIS may cause acute corticosteroid myopathy."}